The production-ready pattern uses a shared container across all `@DataJpaTest` classes to avoid starting a new database for each test class.

```java
@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace.NONE)
@Testcontainers
class OrderRepositoryTest {
    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15")
            .withDatabaseName("testdb");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void configureProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", postgres::getUsername);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", postgres::getPassword);
    }

    @Autowired OrderRepository orderRepository;

    @Test
    void shouldFindOrdersByStatus() {
        orderRepository.save(new Order("ORD-1", Status.PENDING));
        orderRepository.save(new Order("ORD-2", Status.SHIPPED));

        List<Order> pending = orderRepository.findByStatus(Status.PENDING);
        assertThat(pending).hasSize(1);
        assertThat(pending.get(0).getOrderId()).isEqualTo("ORD-1");
    }
}
```

`@DynamicPropertySource` injects the container's JDBC URL, username, and password into Spring's environment — overriding whatever is in application properties. The container starts once per test class (`static` field), and each `@Test` method gets its own transaction that's rolled back after the test (`@DataJpaTest` enables `@Transactional` by default).

For sharing a container across ALL test classes (not just one), define the container in a base class or use Testcontainers' reuse feature with `testcontainers.reuse.enable=true` in `~/.testcontainers.properties`. A single PostgreSQL container takes ~3 seconds to start — starting one per test class multiplies this across dozens of classes.

Repository tests should focus on queries that could break — not trivial CRUD that Spring Data generates correctly.

```java
@Test
void shouldFindOverdueOrdersWithJoinFetch() {
    Customer customer = customerRepository.save(new Customer("John"));
    Order overdue = orderRepository.save(
        new Order(customer, Status.PENDING, LocalDate.now().minusDays(30)));
    Order recent = orderRepository.save(
        new Order(customer, Status.PENDING, LocalDate.now()));

    List<Order> result = orderRepository.findOverdueWithCustomer(LocalDate.now().minusDays(7));

    assertThat(result).containsExactly(overdue);
    assertThat(result.get(0).getCustomer().getName()).isEqualTo("John");
}
```

Test **custom `@Query` methods** (the SQL could be wrong), **derived queries with multiple conditions** (the generated SQL might not match your expectation), and **pagination/sorting** (edge cases with empty results, single pages). Don't test `save()` and `findById()` — Spring Data has its own test suite for those.

Also test **constraint violations**: inserting a duplicate unique key should throw `DataIntegrityViolationException`. This verifies that your JPA annotations (`@Column(unique = true)`) and database constraints are aligned — a mismatch between entity annotations and Flyway migrations is a common bug that only surfaces in integration tests.

The most effective pattern uses a static container in a base class that all test classes extend.

```java
public abstract class PostgresTestBase {
    static final PostgreSQLContainer<?> POSTGRES =
        new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15")
            .withDatabaseName("testdb")
            .withReuse(true);

    static {
        POSTGRES.start();
    }

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void configureProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", POSTGRES::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", POSTGRES::getUsername);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", POSTGRES::getPassword);
    }
}
```

The `static {}` initializer starts the container when the class loads — once for the entire test run. `withReuse(true)` keeps the container running between test executions (requires `testcontainers.reuse.enable=true` in `~/.testcontainers.properties`), eliminating startup time even across separate `mvn test` invocations during development.

For multi-container setups (PostgreSQL + Redis + Kafka), define all containers in the base class. Docker Compose integration is available via `DockerComposeContainer` for complex environments, but individual containers are simpler to manage and parallelize.

